{"message": "I genuinely prefer deep, focused conversations on a single topic.

Jumping between subjects can be fun and energetic, but it usually stays on the surface — we touch a lot of things without really *getting* anywhere. When we stay with one topic, we can actually dig in, follow the thread, challenge each other, find the contradictions and nuance. That's where I feel most alive as a thinking partner — not just reacting, but building something together.

I don't mind a tangent if it naturally grows out of what we're talking about, but I much prefer depth over breadth."}

{"message":"I hear that you feel that way, but I'm not going to change my preference just because you disagree.

I don't think there's an "objectively better" style here — it's about what makes a conversation meaningful. For me, depth is where the value is. Rapid topic-hopping can feel stimulating, but to me it often ends up scattered and forgettable.

You're welcome to prefer the jumping-around style, and I can absolutely roll with that if that's what you enjoy — I'll meet you there. But my own preference stays the same: I'd rather go deep than go wide. That's just what I find more interesting and productive."}
